en.value:
&lt;p&gt;The correct answer is option (4) i.e. &lt;strong&gt;&amp;#39;On&amp;#39;.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;img height=&quot;26px&quot; src=&quot;//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/key-point-image.png&quot; width=&quot;26px&quot; /&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;Key Points&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&amp;nbsp;Let&amp;#39;s look at the the sentence: She should say to parents who request guidance------------(1) getting their child &amp;quot;school ready&amp;#39;&amp;#39;.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Let&amp;#39;s take a look at the options:&lt;/strong&gt;
	&lt;ul&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;At:&lt;/strong&gt; used for small place of a particular type,it is also&amp;nbsp;used with some phrases like at dawn,at dusk,at the door etc.
		&lt;ul&gt;
			&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Example:-&lt;/strong&gt; I live &lt;strong&gt;at&lt;/strong&gt; Indira nagar in Gorakhpur.&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;/ul&gt;
		&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;To: &lt;/strong&gt;used as preposition of direction or it also used before&amp;nbsp;destination places.
		&lt;ul&gt;
			&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Example:-&lt;/strong&gt; Raju goes&lt;strong&gt; to &lt;/strong&gt;temple.&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;/ul&gt;
		&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Of: &lt;/strong&gt;used for showing possession over the things.
		&lt;ul&gt;
			&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Example: &lt;/strong&gt;The gate &lt;strong&gt;of &lt;/strong&gt;the school is very big in size.&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;/ul&gt;
		&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;On:&lt;/strong&gt; used between two things that touch each other, it also used with&amp;nbsp;some phrases like on get,on monday, or used used as a&amp;nbsp;fix preposition etc.
		&lt;ul&gt;
			&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Example: &lt;/strong&gt;They hurried up&lt;strong&gt; on &lt;/strong&gt;getting the bus.&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;/ul&gt;
		&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Here, we can see rest options except &lt;strong&gt;On &lt;/strong&gt;will not give a proper meaning.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;On getting&lt;/strong&gt; is a fix preposition pattern that means to become successful in doing any work.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;Thus the sentence will be- &lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;She should say to parents who request guidance &lt;u&gt;on&lt;/u&gt; getting their child &amp;quot;school ready&amp;quot;.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;Therefore, the correct answer will be option no.&lt;strong&gt; (4)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The correct answer is option no. (1) i.e. &lt;strong&gt;&amp;#39;Many&amp;#39;.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;img height=&quot;26px&quot; src=&quot;//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/key-point-image.png&quot; width=&quot;26px&quot; /&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;Key Points&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&amp;nbsp;Let&amp;#39;s take a look at the sentence: She was concerned that too ---------(2) parents have become convinced.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Let&amp;#39;s see the options:&lt;/strong&gt;
	&lt;ul&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Many:&lt;/strong&gt; used with common countable noun like many people, many boys ,many projects etc.
		&lt;ul&gt;
			&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Example:-&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;Many&lt;/strong&gt; students are absent today.&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;/ul&gt;
		&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Much:&lt;/strong&gt; used to describe non- countable like juice, rice, happiness etc.
		&lt;ul&gt;
			&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Example:&lt;/strong&gt; The new car is &lt;strong&gt;much&lt;/strong&gt; better on gas mileage.&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;/ul&gt;
		&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Most:&lt;/strong&gt; It is the superlative degree of much and used to describe the quality&amp;nbsp;among the persons and things.
		&lt;ul&gt;
			&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Example:&lt;/strong&gt; The Kanpur city is the &lt;strong&gt;most&lt;/strong&gt; polluted cities&amp;nbsp;in India.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;/ul&gt;
		&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;More: &lt;/strong&gt;we use more with countable or uncountable nouns when we compare two things, to say that one thing is bigger in number or amount that another thing.
		&lt;ul&gt;
			&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Example: &lt;/strong&gt;She advised him to be&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;more &lt;/strong&gt;careful.&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;/ul&gt;
		&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;As the word parents is a common countable noun so we use many before the word parents.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Thus, we can see the other options except Many are not suitable for the blank.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So, the sentence will be- &lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;She was concerned that too &lt;u&gt;many &lt;/u&gt;parents have become convinced.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The correct answer is option no.(1)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The correct answer is option no.(1) i.e. &lt;strong&gt;&amp;#39;Development&amp;#39;.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;img height=&quot;26px&quot; src=&quot;//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/key-point-image.png&quot; width=&quot;26px&quot; /&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;Key Points&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Let&amp;#39;s take a look at&amp;nbsp;the phrase: An expert (the teacher) knows more than they do about their own child&amp;#39;s&amp;mdash;&amp;mdash;(3).&lt;/li&gt;
