&lt;p&gt;The correct answer is &amp;#39;&lt;strong&gt;Speak to school groups about the importance of preventing fires&amp;#39;.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;img height=&quot;26px&quot; src=&quot;//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/key-point-image.png&quot; width=&quot;26px&quot; /&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;Key Points&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;The passage discusses the varied responsibilities of firefighters beyond just extinguishing fires. We need to identify the one related to communicating fire prevention.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;The passage mentions firefighters visit schools to teach children how to prevent fires and react if there is a fire. It states they give presentations on topics like smoke alarms and fire escape plans.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;So one responsibility highlighted is to speak to school groups about fire prevention and preparedness. This directly helps in educating children on the importance of preventing fires.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;In summary, the passage cites presentations on fire prevention for school children as one of the public outreach duties of firefighters. This directly conveys the importance of fire prevention.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The correct answer&amp;nbsp;is: &amp;#39;&lt;strong&gt;50% better chance of surviving a fire&amp;#39;.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;img height=&quot;26px&quot; src=&quot;//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/key-point-image.png&quot; width=&quot;26px&quot; /&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;Key Points&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;When a fire occurs, every second counts. The faster a fire can be detected, the more time there is to escape, hence increasing survival rates. Smoke detectors play a crucial role here.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;They can detect smoke particles in the air&amp;mdash;particles which are often present long before the flames are visible or the heat is noticeable&amp;mdash;and trigger an alarm.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;This detection and subsequent alarm allow the occupants a chance to escape before they are at imminent risk.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Moreover, one of the search results specifically states, &amp;quot;Because smoke detectors reduce the risk of dying in a fire by half, smokejumpers often provide audiences with information on how to fix these protective devices in their homes&amp;quot;.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Interpreting this statement, it&amp;#39;s clear that, compared to people without smoke detectors, those who live in homes equipped with smoke detectors have a 50% better chance of surviving a fire.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The correct answer is &amp;#39;&lt;strong&gt;A draft may pull smoke away from the detector&amp;#39;.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;img height=&quot;26px&quot; src=&quot;//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/key-point-image.png&quot; width=&quot;26px&quot; /&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;Key Points&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;The answer as to why smoke detectors should not be installed near windows is that a draft can indeed pull smoke away from the detector.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Smoke detectors work by either detecting visible or invisible particles of combustion, depending on their type (photoelectric or ionization). When a fire starts and smoke reaches the chamber of the detector, it interrupts the flow of current or light beam and triggers the alarm. Therefore, these devices are very reliant on smoke&amp;#39;s physical presence in their detection chambers to activate properly.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;However, if a smoke detector is located near a window, drafts or breezes can divert the path of the smoke, potentially preventing it from reaching the detector&amp;#39;s sensors despite there being a fire. These wind currents caused by open windows, fans, or air conditioning units can move the smoke away, reducing the effectiveness of the detector. This could result in crucial delays to the alarm sounding, thus reducing the time residents have to evacuate or to suppress the fire. This concern becomes particularly significant in the event of smaller, smoldering fires that produce less smoke and are therefore more difficult to detect when smoke dispersion is a factor [0].&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;The placement of detectors is key to their effectiveness. Incorrect placement near windows or ventilation could potentially be as detrimental as not having a detector at all.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The correct answer is&lt;strong&gt; &amp;#39;outside all bedrooms in a home&amp;#39;.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;img height=&quot;26px&quot; src=&quot;//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/key-point-image.png&quot; width=&quot;26px&quot; /&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;Key Points&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Smoke detectors are crucial for early fire detection and can significantly increase chances of survival during a fire. &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;This is because a smoke detector can sense smoke, typically an indicator of fire, triggering an alarm warning those present of the danger.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;In the context of a home, sleeping areas are particularly vulnerable for two reasons. First, fires can occur at night when occupants are likely to be asleep and less aware of their surroundings&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Second, if a fire were to break out in a different part of the house, those sleeping could remain unaware until it&amp;#39;s too late.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Therefore, placing smoke detectors outside all bedrooms, whether they are usually occupied or not (like guest rooms), is a recommended safety requirement.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Apart from outside bedrooms, detectors should also be installed on every level of a house (including the basement) and in every bedroom.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;It&amp;#39;s crucial these alarms are interconnected so that if one alarm sounds, they all do, providing a comprehensive warning system throughout the home&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The correct answer is &lt;strong&gt;The proper installation of home smoke detectors.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;img height=&quot;26px&quot; src=&quot;//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/key-point-image.png&quot; width=&quot;26px&quot; /&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;Key Points&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;The main focus of this passage, as per the texts and discussions presented so far, is indeed the proper installation of home smoke detectors.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The foundation of the conversation emphasizes the crucial role smoke detectors play in home safety. Specifically, these devices serve to alert occupants early in the event of a fire breakout, thereby significantly increasing chances of survival.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;This early detection and alert aren&amp;#39;t reliant merely on the presence of the detectors, but fundamentally on their correct installation&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Accurate placement of these detectors is key to their performance, with ideal locations being outside all bedrooms and on each level of a home, among others. &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Suboptimal placement can result in delayed or even missed smoke detection, which, in turn, decreases the available time to escape, directly impacting occupants&amp;#39; safety.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Finally, the conversation outlines conditions to avoid during detector placement, such as near windows, where drafts can divert smoke away from the detector, potentially affecting its alarm triggering.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Overall, the collective aim of the discussion heads towards creating awareness around the importance of correctly installing smoke detectors to maximize their utility, which can make a critical difference in the outcome of a home fire.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The correct answer is&amp;nbsp;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Dennis Gabor&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;img height=&quot;26px&quot; src=&quot;//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/key-point-image.png&quot; width=&quot;26px&quot; /&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;Key Points&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Dennis Gabor, &lt;/strong&gt;a &lt;strong&gt;Hungarian-born British scientist,&lt;/strong&gt; is renowned for pioneering the &lt;strong&gt;principle of holography in 1947&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;His primary objective was to enhance the image resolution of electron microscopes, which, at the time, struggled to produce sharply focused images. G&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;abor&amp;#39;s groundbreaking work in holography earned him the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1971.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Fleeing from Nazi Germany in 1933 due to his Jewish heritage, Gabor found refuge in Britain and was invited to work at the development department of the British Thomson-Houston company in Rugby, Warwickshire.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;He obtained British citizenship in 1934 and spent the majority of his life in England.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;img height=&quot;26px&quot; src=&quot;//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/important-point-image.png&quot; width=&quot;26px&quot; /&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;Important Points&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Werner Karl Heisenberg&lt;/strong&gt;, a German theoretical physicist, was a key figure in the development of quantum mechanics. His seminal work was published in 1925, marking a major breakthrough in the field.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lloyd Cross&lt;/strong&gt;, an American physicist and holographer, began his research in the 1950s, focusing primarily on masers and lasers at Willow Run Laboratories, University of Michigan.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Charles Hard Townes, &lt;/strong&gt;an American physicist, conducted influential research in quantum electronics.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

